// Class 11 Political Science practice bank, part 1: Indian Constitution at Work, chapters 1-6 (Constitution to Judiciary).
StudyData.addPractice(11, "polsci", {
  "constitution-why-and-how": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The Constitution of India was adopted by the Constituent Assembly on:", options: ["26 January 1950", "26 November 1949", "15 August 1947", "9 December 1946"], answer: 1, explain: "It was adopted on 26 November 1949 (observed as Constitution Day) and came into force on 26 January 1950.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The Constituent Assembly was formed broadly under the plan of which year?", options: ["1942", "1947", "1950", "1946"], answer: 3, explain: "The Cabinet Mission Plan of 1946 laid down how the Assembly would be composed.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Who was elected President of the Constituent Assembly?", options: ["Dr B.R. Ambedkar", "Jawaharlal Nehru", "Dr Rajendra Prasad", "Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel"], answer: 2, explain: "Dr Rajendra Prasad presided over the Assembly; Ambedkar chaired the Drafting Committee.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The Speaker as an institution and the first-past-the-post method of election were drawn from the constitutional practice of:", options: ["the USA", "Ireland", "Britain", "France"], answer: 2, explain: "The parliamentary system, FPTP elections, rule of law and the Speaker came from Britain.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which ideals in the Indian Constitution were drawn from the French Constitution?", options: ["Judicial review and Fundamental Rights", "Directive Principles", "Residuary powers with the Centre", "Liberty, equality and fraternity"], answer: 3, explain: "Liberty, equality and fraternity came from France.", level: 1 },

      { q: "A constitution imposed on a colony by a foreign ruler is likely to lack legitimacy mainly because of weakness in its:", options: ["balanced institutional design", "mode of promulgation", "number of Fundamental Rights", "amendment procedure"], answer: 1, explain: "Mode of promulgation asks who framed the constitution and with what authority; a body of popular, national leaders gives it legitimacy.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Out of the originally planned 389 members of the Constituent Assembly, 93 were to come from the princely states. How many were to come from the provinces and Chief Commissioner's provinces together?", options: ["292", "296", "299", "389"], answer: 1, explain: "389 − 93 = 296 (292 from provinces and 4 from Chief Commissioner's provinces).", level: 2 },
      { q: "The Objectives Resolution, moved on 13 December 1946, was adopted by the Constituent Assembly on:", options: ["26 November 1949", "29 August 1947", "22 January 1947", "26 January 1950"], answer: 2, explain: "Nehru moved it on 13 December 1946; it was adopted on 22 January 1947 and later shaped the Preamble.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Case: Meena reads that India has a federation with a strong Centre and that all powers not listed in any list rest with the Union. She wants to know which country's constitution influenced this idea. The answer is:", options: ["Canada", "Ireland", "Britain", "France"], answer: 0, explain: "Quasi-federalism with a strong Centre and the idea of residuary powers came from Canada.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): The Indian Constitution cannot be called a mere copy of other constitutions.\nReason (R): The makers adapted the ideas they borrowed to suit Indian needs and also drew on the national movement.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "Borrowed ideas were adapted, and many provisions came from the national movement and the Government of India Act, 1935, so the Constitution is not a copy.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },

      {
        q: "Assertion (A): A constitution is respected more when power is divided among institutions.\nReason (R): Balanced institutional design stops any one organ or group from subverting the constitution.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "Division of power with checks and balances is one source of a constitution's authority because it prevents misuse of power by a single group.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: In a country, a small group of unelected persons drafts a constitution and gives all power to a single body, with no other institution to check it, though it lists many rights. Which sources of authority are weak here?", options: ["Only substantive provisions", "Only the amendment procedure", "Mode of promulgation and balanced institutional design", "No source, since many rights are listed"], answer: 2, explain: "It was framed without popular authority (mode of promulgation) and concentrates power (no balance), so listed rights may not be secure.", level: 3 },
      { q: "In the Constituent Assembly, seats were given roughly at one per 10 lakh of population. A province with a population of 3 crore would get about how many seats, and how were its community-wise members elected?", options: ["30; by plurality in single-member areas", "300; by direct adult vote", "30; by proportional representation with single transferable vote", "3; by nomination of the Governor"], answer: 2, explain: "3 crore = 30 million, so about 30 seats; each community's members in the provincial assembly elected its representatives by PR with STV.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Which is the correct chronological order of these events?", options: ["Drafting Committee set up; Objectives Resolution moved; Constitution adopted; Constitution comes into force", "Objectives Resolution moved; Drafting Committee set up; Constitution adopted; Constitution comes into force", "Objectives Resolution moved; Constitution adopted; Drafting Committee set up; Constitution comes into force", "First meeting of the Assembly; Constitution adopted; Objectives Resolution moved; Drafting Committee set up"], answer: 1, explain: "Objectives Resolution (13 Dec 1946), Drafting Committee (29 Aug 1947), adoption (26 Nov 1949), commencement (26 Jan 1950).", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Name the plan under which the Constituent Assembly of India was formed.", a: "The Cabinet Mission Plan of 1946.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "How much time did the Constituent Assembly take to frame the Constitution?", a: "2 years, 11 months and 18 days.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "What is meant by the 'mode of promulgation' of a constitution?", a: "Mode of promulgation means how a constitution came into being: who framed it and with what authority.\nA constitution framed by popular national leaders through a representative body gets more legitimacy.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Mention two ways in which the national movement influenced the framing of the Constitution.", a: "1. Earlier demands for rights and democracy, such as the Motilal Nehru Report (1928) and the Karachi Resolution of the Congress (1931) on fundamental rights, were carried into the Constitution.\n2. The ideals of the movement (justice, liberty, equality, safeguards for minorities) were put in the Objectives Resolution, which shaped the Preamble.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Why does a constitution place limits on the powers of the government? Explain.", a: "1. Without limits a government could impose its will on citizens arbitrarily.\n2. Limits, mainly through Fundamental Rights, protect the freedom and equality of citizens.\n3. They assure people that even a majority government cannot take away basic rights, which builds trust in the system.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "How did the working of the Constituent Assembly reflect deliberation and consensus?", a: "1. Decisions were reached through open discussion and reasoned debate.\n2. Members tried to work by consensus rather than relying on narrow majorities.\n3. Committees prepared drafts and the debates were recorded, so the reasoning behind each provision can be traced.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Name the countries from which India drew the following, and state one adaptation made: (a) Directive Principles, (b) Fundamental Rights and judicial review, (c) parliamentary system.", a: "(a) Directive Principles: Ireland.\n(b) Fundamental Rights and judicial review: the USA.\n(c) Parliamentary system: Britain.\nIdeas were adapted to Indian conditions, for example a federation with a strong Centre for a diverse country.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Explain the sources from which a constitution derives its authority.", a: "1. Mode of promulgation: a constitution framed by popular national leaders through a representative body enjoys legitimacy.\n2. Substantive provisions: it earns respect when it gives all reasons to support it, e.g. by protecting freedom and equality.\n3. Balanced institutional design: power is divided so that no organ or group can subvert the constitution.\n4. It is neither too rigid nor too easy to change, so it can be amended for new circumstances.\n5. Together these make people see the constitution as their own and willingly follow it.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Describe how the national movement, the Objectives Resolution and earlier colonial laws shaped the Indian Constitution.", a: "1. The national movement had demanded rights and democracy, e.g. the Motilal Nehru Report (1928) and the Karachi Resolution (1931).\n2. The Objectives Resolution, moved by Nehru on 13 December 1946, set the aims of an independent sovereign republic, justice, equality, freedom and safeguards for minorities.\n3. The resolution was adopted on 22 January 1947 and later shaped the Preamble.\n4. Many institutional details were taken from colonial laws, especially the Government of India Act, 1935.\n5. The makers combined these inheritances with ideas from other constitutions, adapting them to Indian needs.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: The Constituent Assembly first met on 9 December 1946. Its members were chosen by the members of provincial assemblies, which had been elected on a limited vote, and the Assembly worked with eight major committees. The Drafting Committee was set up on 29 August 1947 under Dr B.R. Ambedkar. The Constitution was adopted on 26 November 1949.\n(i) Who chaired the Drafting Committee?\n(ii) How were the members of the Assembly chosen?\n(iii) Give one reason why the Assembly's debates are useful to us today.", a: "(i) Dr B.R. Ambedkar.\n(ii) Indirectly, by members of the provincial legislative assemblies, using proportional representation with single transferable vote.\n(iii) The debates were recorded, so the reasoning behind each provision can be traced and used to interpret the Constitution.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "rights-in-the-indian-constitution": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The Fundamental Rights are contained in which part of the Constitution?", options: ["Part II", "Part III", "Part IV", "Part IVA"], answer: 1, explain: "Part III (Articles 12–35) contains the Fundamental Rights.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Free and compulsory education for children aged 6–14 years is a Fundamental Right under:", options: ["Article 14", "Article 19", "Article 24", "Article 21A"], answer: 3, explain: "Article 21A was added by the 86th Amendment (2002).", level: 1 },
      { q: "Article 24 prohibits the employment of children below the age of how many years in factories, mines and other hazardous work?", options: ["14", "16", "18", "12"], answer: 0, explain: "Article 24 bans employing children below 14 years in hazardous work.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A writ issued to secure the release of a person who has been unlawfully detained is:", options: ["Mandamus", "Certiorari", "Habeas corpus", "Prohibition"], answer: 2, explain: "Habeas corpus orders that the detained person be produced before the court, which can set them free if the detention is illegal.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The Fundamental Duties are contained in:", options: ["Part IV", "Part IVA (Article 51A)", "Part III", "Part V"], answer: 1, explain: "Fundamental Duties are in Part IVA, Article 51A, added by the 42nd Amendment (1976).", level: 1 },

      { q: "Which of the following is NOT a ground on which Article 15 prohibits discrimination?", options: ["Religion", "Caste", "Place of birth", "Level of income"], answer: 3, explain: "Article 15 lists religion, race, caste, sex and place of birth; income is not listed.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Which Fundamental Right is also enforceable against private individuals, not only against the state?", options: ["Article 14", "Article 17 (abolition of untouchability)", "Article 20", "Article 32"], answer: 1, explain: "The bans on untouchability and on forced labour apply to private persons too; most rights protect against state action.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Case: Ravi is arrested on a Monday evening and kept in a police station until Wednesday morning without being taken to a magistrate. Which provision is violated and which writ can his family seek?", options: ["Article 22; habeas corpus", "Article 24; mandamus", "Article 19; quo warranto", "Article 17; certiorari"], answer: 0, explain: "Article 22 requires production before the nearest magistrate within 24 hours; habeas corpus gets the detained person produced before the court.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Fundamental Rights in India are not absolute.\nReason (R): The government can impose reasonable restrictions on them, for example in the interest of public order.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "Rights such as freedom of speech are subject to reasonable restrictions for public order, security of the state, decency and so on.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: A government school maintained wholly out of state funds makes the prayers of one religion compulsory for all students. Which provision does this go against?", options: ["Article 30, which protects minority institutions", "Article 16, which deals with public employment", "Article 28, which bars religious instruction in institutions wholly maintained by the state", "Article 24, which bans child labour"], answer: 2, explain: "Articles 25–28 include the rule that no religious instruction is given in institutions wholly maintained by state funds.", level: 2 },

      { q: "Which of the following is a correct difference between Fundamental Rights and Directive Principles?", options: ["Both are enforceable in courts", "Directive Principles are justiciable, Fundamental Rights are not", "Fundamental Rights restrict the government, Directive Principles urge it to act", "Directive Principles are in Part III"], answer: 2, explain: "Fundamental Rights are justiciable limits on the state (Part III); Directive Principles (Part IV) are non-justiciable guidelines.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Case: A person who has not met the required qualifications for a public office starts functioning in it. Which writ can a court issue to stop him from holding the office?", options: ["Mandamus", "Quo warranto", "Habeas corpus", "Certiorari"], answer: 1, explain: "Quo warranto stops a person from holding a public office to which he is not entitled.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Which statement is correct?", options: ["Fundamental Rights can be changed by an ordinary law", "Fundamental Duties are directly enforceable in courts", "Directive Principles can be enforced under Article 32", "A person can approach the Supreme Court directly under Article 32 and the High Court under Article 226"], answer: 3, explain: "Article 32 gives direct access to the Supreme Court; High Courts have similar writ power under Article 226. Rights need a constitutional amendment to change; Duties and DPSPs are not directly enforceable.", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Directive Principles are enforceable by courts in the same way as Fundamental Rights.\nReason (R): Directive Principles are guidelines for the government while making laws and policies.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 3, explain: "Directive Principles are non-justiciable, so A is false; R correctly describes their role.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Which Article guarantees equality before law and equal protection of the laws?", a: "Article 14.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Which constitutional amendment made free and compulsory education for children aged 6–14 a Fundamental Right?", a: "The 86th Amendment (2002), which added Article 21A.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Distinguish between the writs of habeas corpus and mandamus.", a: "1. Habeas corpus: the court orders that an arrested person be produced before it and frees the person if the detention is unlawful.\n2. Mandamus: the court orders a public official to perform a legal duty that he is not performing.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "State two protections available to an arrested person under Article 22.", a: "1. The person must be told the grounds of arrest and may consult a lawyer of his choice.\n2. The person must be produced before the nearest magistrate within 24 hours of arrest.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Explain the Right against Exploitation.", a: "1. Article 23 bans traffic in human beings and begar (forced labour without payment) and other forms of forced labour.\n2. Article 24 bans the employment of children below 14 years in factories, mines and other hazardous work.\n3. Together they protect weaker sections, such as the poor and children, from being exploited.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Give three examples of Directive Principles of State Policy.", a: "1. Adequate means of livelihood and equal pay for equal work for men and women (Article 39).\n2. Organisation of village panchayats (Article 40).\n3. Protection and improvement of the environment (Article 48A); others are a uniform civil code (Article 44) and separation of the judiciary from the executive (Article 50).", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Why are Fundamental Rights not absolute? Give examples of restrictions.", a: "1. Rights of one person may clash with those of others or with the interest of society, so some limits are needed.\n2. The government can impose reasonable restrictions, for example for public order, security of the state or decency.\n3. For example, freedom of speech does not allow incitement to violence, and freedom of assembly must be peaceful and without arms.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Explain the Right to Equality (Articles 14–18).", a: "1. Article 14: equality before law and equal protection of laws for all persons.\n2. Article 15: no discrimination on grounds of religion, race, caste, sex or place of birth, including access to shops, hotels, wells and bathing ghats.\n3. Article 16: equality of opportunity in public employment; the state may make reservations for backward classes.\n4. Article 17: untouchability is abolished and its practice is a punishable offence.\n5. Article 18: titles are abolished; only military and academic distinctions may be conferred.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Explain the Right to Freedom under Articles 19–22.", a: "1. Article 19 gives citizens six freedoms: speech and expression; peaceful assembly without arms; forming associations and unions; moving freely throughout India; residing and settling anywhere in India; practising any profession, occupation, trade or business.\n2. Article 20: no punishment under a law made after the act, no punishment twice for the same offence, and no one can be compelled to be a witness against himself.\n3. Article 21: no one can be deprived of life or personal liberty except according to procedure established by law; courts have widened it to livelihood, shelter, clean environment and privacy.\n4. Article 21A: free and compulsory education for children aged 6–14.\n5. Article 22: safeguards for arrested persons (grounds, lawyer, magistrate within 24 hours), with preventive detention allowed under safeguards.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: Anita, aged 12, works in a roadside factory in her town and is paid very little. A local teacher reads about this and wants to help her. He says the law forbids such work and that she has a right to education.\n(i) Which Fundamental Right is violated by her employment?\n(ii) Where can a person go to enforce a Fundamental Right directly?\n(iii) Which Article gives Anita a right to education?", a: "(i) Article 24 (Right against Exploitation), which bans employing children below 14 years in factories and hazardous work.\n(ii) Directly to the Supreme Court under Article 32, or to the High Court under Article 226.\n(iii) Article 21A, which gives free and compulsory education to children aged 6–14.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "election-and-representation": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The First Past the Post system is also known as the:", options: ["list system", "plurality system", "single transferable vote system", "quota system"], answer: 1, explain: "The candidate with the most votes (a plurality) wins, even without a majority.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which Article sets up the Election Commission of India?", options: ["Article 324", "Article 326", "Article 110", "Article 32"], answer: 0, explain: "Article 324 provides for an independent Election Commission to superintend, direct and control elections.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The minimum age for contesting a Rajya Sabha election is:", options: ["21 years", "25 years", "30 years", "35 years"], answer: 2, explain: "It is 25 years for the Lok Sabha and Assemblies, and 30 years for the Rajya Sabha.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Universal adult franchise is guaranteed by:", options: ["Article 326", "Article 324", "Article 19", "Article 14"], answer: 0, explain: "Article 326 provides for elections on the basis of adult suffrage; the voting age is 18.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Since 1993 the Election Commission of India has had how many members?", options: ["One", "Two", "Five", "Three"], answer: 3, explain: "It has the Chief Election Commissioner and two Election Commissioners.", level: 1 },

      { q: "In a state with 300 MLAs, 5 Rajya Sabha seats are to be filled by single transferable vote. The quota needed to win a seat is:", options: ["50", "51", "60", "61"], answer: 1, explain: "Quota = 300 ÷ (5 + 1) + 1 = 50 + 1 = 51.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Case: In a constituency the votes polled were: A 40,000; B 35,000; C 30,000; D 15,000 (total 1,20,000). Under the system used for the Lok Sabha, which statement is correct?", options: ["A wins with an absolute majority", "A wins with one-third of the votes, without a majority", "No one wins because nobody has a majority", "A second round is held between A and B"], answer: 1, explain: "A has the most votes and wins under FPTP; 40,000 of 1,20,000 is one-third, not a majority.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Which of the following elections uses proportional representation by single transferable vote?", options: ["Lok Sabha", "Legislative Assembly", "Vice-President", "Gram Panchayat"], answer: 2, explain: "PR by STV is used for Rajya Sabha, President, Vice-President and Legislative Councils.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): In a reserved constituency only SC or ST candidates can contest.\nReason (R): Only SC or ST voters of that constituency are allowed to vote there.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 2, explain: "All voters of the constituency vote; only the candidates are restricted to SC or ST.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: After a census, the boundaries of Lok Sabha seats in a state are redrawn and some seats are marked for Scheduled Castes. Which body does this work?", options: ["The Finance Commission", "The Delimitation Commission, appointed by the President", "The State Public Service Commission", "The Speaker of the Lok Sabha"], answer: 1, explain: "The Delimitation Commission draws constituency boundaries and decides which seats are reserved.", level: 2 },

      { q: "Which of the following is NOT a reason for India adopting the FPTP system?", options: ["It is simple for ordinary voters to understand", "It gives voters a clear choice and an accountable representative", "It usually produces stable governments", "It gives each party seats exactly in proportion to its votes"], answer: 3, explain: "Exact proportion between votes and seats is a feature of PR, not FPTP.", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Under FPTP a party can win a larger share of seats than its share of votes.\nReason (R): In each constituency only the candidate with the most votes wins, and the votes for the others do not earn seats.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "Winning many seats narrowly while losing others by large margins creates the gap between votes and seats.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "In a state with 240 MLAs, 3 Rajya Sabha members are to be elected. The quota is:", options: ["60", "80", "61", "81"], answer: 2, explain: "Quota = 240 ÷ (3 + 1) + 1 = 60 + 1 = 61.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Case: The Chief Election Commissioner orders a re-poll in some booths, which the ruling party dislikes. Which constitutional protection stops the government from simply removing him?", options: ["He is appointed by the Chief Justice of India", "State Election Commissions can overrule him", "He holds office at the pleasure of the Prime Minister", "He can be removed only in the same manner as a Supreme Court judge"], answer: 3, explain: "Removal needs a special majority in both Houses, and his service conditions cannot be changed to his disadvantage.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Who appoints the Chief Election Commissioner and the other Election Commissioners?", a: "The President of India.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Name the electoral system used for elections to the Lok Sabha.", a: "First Past the Post (plurality system), in single-member constituencies.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "What is universal adult franchise? Which amendment reduced the voting age to 18?", a: "1. It is the right of every adult citizen to vote, without discrimination.\n2. The 61st Amendment (1988) lowered the voting age from 21 to 18 years.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "What is a reserved constituency? Who can contest and who can vote in it?", a: "1. It is a constituency set aside for candidates belonging to the Scheduled Castes or the Scheduled Tribes.\n2. Only SC (or ST) candidates can contest, but all voters of the constituency vote.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Why was the proportional representation system not adopted for the Lok Sabha? Give three reasons.", a: "1. PR tends to produce many parties and unstable coalition governments.\n2. It is more complex for voters with little experience of politics and elections.\n3. FPTP gives voters a clear choice of candidates and a representative accountable to a particular area, and it encourages different social groups to come together.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "State three functions of the Election Commission of India.", a: "1. It prepares and updates the electoral rolls.\n2. It fixes the election schedule and enforces the Model Code of Conduct, and can order re-polls.\n3. It recognises political parties and allots election symbols.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "In an Assembly of 150 MLAs, two Rajya Sabha seats are to be filled by single transferable vote. Find the quota and explain how voters vote.", a: "1. Quota = (150 ÷ (2 + 1)) + 1 = 50 + 1 = 51 votes.\n2. A candidate who reaches 51 votes is elected.\n3. Voters rank candidates in order of preference, and votes are transferred according to those preferences.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Compare the First Past the Post and Proportional Representation systems of election.", a: "1. Constituencies: FPTP has single-member constituencies; PR has large constituencies electing several members.\n2. Vote: in FPTP voters choose a candidate; in the list system they vote for a party.\n3. Winning: in FPTP the candidate with the most votes wins even without a majority; in PR parties get seats in proportion to their votes.\n4. Results: FPTP can give a party more or fewer seats than its vote share; PR matches seats to votes closely.\n5. Governments: FPTP usually produces stable governments; PR often leads to many parties and coalitions.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Explain the system of reservation in Lok Sabha and Assembly elections, including the role of the Delimitation Commission.", a: "1. Seats are reserved for Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes in the Lok Sabha and State Assemblies.\n2. In a reserved seat all voters vote, but only SC (or ST) candidates can contest.\n3. The Delimitation Commission, appointed by the President, draws constituency boundaries and decides which seats are reserved, based on the share of SC/ST population.\n4. The 104th Amendment (2019) extended this reservation to 2030.\n5. The 106th Amendment (2023) provides for one-third of seats in the Lok Sabha and Assemblies to be reserved for women, to take effect after the next delimitation.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: In a constituency, three candidates receive the following share of votes: A 38%, B 33% and C 29%. The returning officer declares A elected.\n(i) Who wins and why?\n(ii) Does the winner have a majority of the votes?\n(iii) Name the electoral system and state one of its advantages.", a: "(i) A wins because A has the highest number of votes.\n(ii) No. 38% is less than 50%, so A won without a majority.\n(iii) First Past the Post (plurality system). Advantage: it is simple for voters and usually gives stable governments (or gives an accountable local representative).", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "executive": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The permanent executive in India consists of:", options: ["the Prime Minister and ministers", "the President alone", "civil servants", "members of Parliament"], answer: 2, explain: "Civil servants (the bureaucracy) stay in office when governments change, so they form the permanent executive.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The President of India holds office for a term of:", options: ["4 years", "5 years", "6 years", "7 years"], answer: 1, explain: "The President's term is 5 years (Article 56).", level: 1 },
      { q: "The Council of Ministers is collectively responsible to the Lok Sabha under:", options: ["Article 52", "Article 53", "Article 75", "Article 123"], answer: 2, explain: "Article 75 provides for appointment of the PM and ministers and their collective responsibility to the Lok Sabha.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The President can issue an ordinance under:", options: ["Article 123", "Article 72", "Article 52", "Article 53"], answer: 0, explain: "Article 123 allows ordinances when Parliament is not in session; Article 72 is the pardoning power.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Who is the ex officio Chairman of the Rajya Sabha?", options: ["The Speaker", "The Prime Minister", "The Chief Justice of India", "The Vice-President"], answer: 3, explain: "The Vice-President is the ex officio Chairman of the Rajya Sabha.", level: 1 },

      { q: "Which of the following takes part in the election of the President of India?", options: ["Nominated members of the Rajya Sabha", "Elected members of State Legislative Councils", "Elected members of State Legislative Assemblies", "Members of panchayats"], answer: 2, explain: "The electoral college has the elected MPs and the elected MLAs of the states (including Delhi and Puducherry).", level: 2 },
      { q: "Case: In a country the head of state is also the head of government, is elected separately from the legislature and does not need a legislative majority to remain in office. This is a:", options: ["parliamentary system", "semi-presidential system", "constitutional monarchy", "presidential system"], answer: 3, explain: "This describes the presidential system, as in the USA.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Under Article 74(1) as amended by the 44th Amendment, if the Council of Ministers sends back the same advice after the President has asked for reconsideration, the President:", options: ["may reject it again", "must accept it", "may dismiss the Council of Ministers", "may refer it to the Supreme Court"], answer: 1, explain: "The President can return advice once but must act on the reconsidered advice.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): The Council of Ministers must resign if the Lok Sabha passes a no-confidence motion against it.\nReason (R): The Council of Ministers is collectively responsible to the Rajya Sabha.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 2, explain: "The Council of Ministers is responsible to the Lok Sabha, not to the Rajya Sabha.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: Mr Rao is not a member of Parliament but is appointed a Union minister. For how long can he continue as a minister without becoming an MP?", options: ["Three months", "One year", "Until the next general election", "Six months"], answer: 3, explain: "A minister must be an MP or become one within six months.", level: 2 },

      { q: "The Lok Sabha has 543 elected members. Under the 91st Amendment, the largest whole number of ministers (including the PM) in the Union Council of Ministers can be:", options: ["81", "80", "82", "91"], answer: 0, explain: "15% of 543 = 81.45, and the limit cannot be exceeded, so the maximum is 81.", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): In normal times the President of India is a nominal head of the executive.\nReason (R): Under Article 74(1) the President acts on the advice of the Council of Ministers headed by the Prime Minister.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "Because the President must act on ministerial advice, real executive power rests with the Council of Ministers.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "No party or alliance has a clear majority in the Lok Sabha after an election. The President:", options: ["must appoint the leader of the largest party without any judgment", "uses judgment to appoint the person who can command majority support", "dissolves the Lok Sabha at once", "asks the Chief Justice to choose the Prime Minister"], answer: 1, explain: "In a hung parliament the President uses judgment in deciding whom to appoint as PM.", level: 3 },
      { q: "The President can be impeached only if the motion is passed by:", options: ["a simple majority of the Lok Sabha", "two-thirds of members present and voting in the Lok Sabha", "two-thirds of the total membership of each House of Parliament", "a majority of Supreme Court judges"], answer: 2, explain: "Impeachment is for violation of the Constitution and needs a two-thirds majority of the total membership of each House.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "What is a pocket veto?", a: "It is the President's power to keep an ordinary bill pending indefinitely, neither giving nor refusing assent.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Who is the head of government in India's parliamentary system?", a: "The Prime Minister.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Distinguish between the political executive and the permanent executive.", a: "1. The political executive consists of elected leaders (PM and ministers) who decide overall policy and hold office for a limited period.\n2. The permanent executive consists of civil servants who carry out policies and continue in office even when governments change.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "State two differences between the presidential and parliamentary systems of government.", a: "1. In the presidential system the President is both head of state and head of government and is elected separately; in the parliamentary system the PM is head of government and the President or monarch is a ceremonial head of state.\n2. A presidential executive does not depend on a majority in the legislature; a parliamentary executive must keep the support of a majority in the legislature.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Explain how the President of India is elected.", a: "1. The election is indirect, by an electoral college of elected MPs and elected MLAs of the states (including Delhi and Puducherry).\n2. It is held by proportional representation with the single transferable vote.\n3. The President serves for five years and can be removed only by impeachment for violating the Constitution.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Describe the position and role of the Vice-President of India.", a: "1. The Vice-President is elected for five years by members of both Houses of Parliament.\n2. He is the ex officio Chairman of the Rajya Sabha.\n3. He acts as President if the office falls vacant, until a new President is elected (within six months).", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Explain the principle of collective responsibility. What limit did the 91st Amendment place on the size of the Council of Ministers?", a: "1. The whole Council of Ministers is answerable to the Lok Sabha and stands or falls together.\n2. If the Lok Sabha passes a no-confidence motion, the entire ministry must resign.\n3. The 91st Amendment (2003) limits the Council of Ministers to 15% of the total strength of the Lok Sabha (or of the Assembly in states).", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Describe the powers of the President of India, including his discretionary powers.", a: "1. Executive: he appoints the PM, other ministers, judges and Governors, though on the advice of the Council of Ministers.\n2. Legislative: he can issue ordinances (Article 123) when Parliament is not in session; assent is needed for a bill to become law.\n3. Pardoning power under Article 72.\n4. Discretion: he can return the advice of the Council of Ministers once for reconsideration, and has a pocket veto over ordinary bills (e.g. the Indian Post Office (Amendment) Bill, 1986).\n5. In a hung parliament he uses his judgment in deciding whom to appoint as PM.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Explain the role of the bureaucracy as the permanent executive. Mention its concerns.", a: "1. Civil servants implement government policies and are expected to be politically neutral and professionally skilled.\n2. The UPSC recruits officers for the IAS, IPS and other central services; State Public Service Commissions recruit for state services.\n3. IAS and IPS belong to the All India Services: they serve under states, but the Union controls recruitment and discipline.\n4. Reservations for SCs, STs and OBCs make the bureaucracy more representative.\n5. Concerns include red tape, weak accountability to citizens and political interference; the Right to Information Act (2005) helps citizens question officials.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: After a general election to the 543-member Lok Sabha, no party has won more than 130 seats. A group of parties led by party A tells the President that it has the support of 285 members.\n(i) What is such a situation called?\n(ii) Why can the President invite the leader of party A to form the government?\n(iii) What must the Council of Ministers do if the Lok Sabha later passes a no-confidence motion against it?", a: "(i) A hung parliament.\n(ii) A majority in a 543-member House is 272 members; with 285 members the group has majority support, so the President can appoint its leader as PM.\n(iii) The whole Council of Ministers must resign, because it is collectively responsible to the Lok Sabha.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "legislature": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "According to Article 79, Parliament of India consists of:", options: ["the Lok Sabha and the Rajya Sabha only", "the President, the Rajya Sabha and the Lok Sabha", "the Prime Minister, the Lok Sabha and the Rajya Sabha", "the President, the Vice-President and the Speaker"], answer: 1, explain: "Parliament = President + Rajya Sabha + Lok Sabha.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The maximum strength of the Rajya Sabha is:", options: ["100", "245", "543", "250"], answer: 3, explain: "It can have at most 250 members, 12 of whom are nominated by the President.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A member of the Rajya Sabha is elected for a term of:", options: ["4 years", "5 years", "6 years", "7 years"], answer: 2, explain: "The term is 6 years, with one-third of members retiring every two years.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The Lok Sabha is presided over by:", options: ["the Vice-President", "the Speaker", "the Prime Minister", "the President"], answer: 1, explain: "The Speaker is elected by the members of the Lok Sabha from among themselves.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A Money Bill can be introduced only in the:", options: ["Lok Sabha", "Rajya Sabha", "either House", "a joint sitting"], answer: 0, explain: "Under Article 110, Money Bills originate only in the Lok Sabha.", level: 1 },

      { q: "How are seats in the Rajya Sabha distributed among the states?", options: ["Equally for every state", "By the number of districts", "Roughly in proportion to population", "By the area of the state"], answer: 2, explain: "Uttar Pradesh has far more seats than Sikkim; in the US Senate every state has equal seats.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Case: The Lok Sabha passes an ordinary bill but the Rajya Sabha rejects it. What can happen next?", options: ["The President can call a joint sitting, presided over by the Speaker", "The bill lapses at once and cannot be reconsidered", "The Rajya Sabha's decision is final", "The Supreme Court decides the bill's fate"], answer: 0, explain: "Article 108 provides for a joint sitting to resolve the deadlock on an ordinary bill; the larger Lok Sabha usually prevails.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Under the Tenth Schedule, a member of a House who is held to have defected is disqualified by:", options: ["the Election Commission", "the President", "the Chief Justice of India", "the presiding officer of that House"], answer: 3, explain: "The Speaker (in the Lok Sabha) or the Chairman (in the Rajya Sabha) decides disqualification.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): The Rajya Sabha is a permanent House.\nReason (R): Its members are directly elected by the people for five years.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 2, explain: "The Rajya Sabha is never dissolved; its members serve 6 years and are elected indirectly by MLAs.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: An MP wishes to raise an urgent matter of public importance in the House soon after the question session. This time in the proceedings is called:", options: ["Question Hour", "Zero Hour", "Joint sitting", "Budget debate"], answer: 1, explain: "Zero Hour is when members raise urgent matters, usually after Question Hour.", level: 2 },

      { q: "Under Article 249, a resolution of the Rajya Sabha that lets Parliament legislate on a State List subject must be passed by:", options: ["a simple majority of the total membership", "two-thirds of the members present and voting", "two-thirds of the total membership", "all members present"], answer: 1, explain: "Article 249 requires two-thirds of members present and voting.", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Joint sittings of Parliament have been held very frequently to pass bills.\nReason (R): In a joint sitting the Lok Sabha, being the larger House, usually prevails.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 3, explain: "Joint sittings have been held only three times (1961, 1978, 2002), so A is false; R is true.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: A party has 45 legislators in a House. Under the anti-defection law as amended in 2003, what is the minimum number who must agree to merge with another party so that they escape disqualification?", options: ["23", "31", "30", "34"], answer: 2, explain: "A merger needs at least two-thirds of the party's legislators: 2/3 of 45 = 30.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Which is the correct order of stages for an ordinary government bill?", options: ["President's assent; introduction; discussion in the other House; committee", "Discussion in the other House; introduction; committee; assent", "Introduction; assent; committee; discussion in the other House", "Introduction; committee study and debate; passage and discussion in the other House; President's assent"], answer: 3, explain: "A bill is introduced, studied and debated, passed, sent to the other House, and becomes an Act after the President's assent (Article 111).", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Who decides whether a bill is a Money Bill?", a: "The Speaker of the Lok Sabha.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "How many times have the two Houses held a joint sitting to pass a bill?", a: "Three times: on the Dowry Prohibition Bill (1961), the Banking Service Commission (Repeal) Bill (1978) and the Prevention of Terrorism Bill (2002).", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Differentiate between a government bill and a private member's bill.", a: "1. A government bill is introduced by a minister.\n2. A private member's bill is introduced by any member who is not a minister.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "What are Question Hour and Zero Hour?", a: "1. Question Hour is the time when members ask ministers questions about the working of government.\n2. Zero Hour is the time, usually after Question Hour, when members raise urgent matters of public importance.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Explain two special powers of the Rajya Sabha and why it is called a permanent House.", a: "1. Under Article 249 it can, by a two-thirds vote of members present and voting, let Parliament make laws on a State List subject.\n2. Under Article 312 it can approve the creation of new All India Services.\n3. It is permanent because it is never dissolved: members serve six years and one-third retire every two years.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Why was the anti-defection law introduced, and how was it tightened in 2003?", a: "1. The 52nd Amendment (1985) added the Tenth Schedule to stop defections driven by the lure of office or money.\n2. A member found guilty of defection can be disqualified by the presiding officer of the House.\n3. The 91st Amendment (2003) removed the protection for a 'split'; a merger is allowed only if at least two-thirds of a party's legislators agree.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "How do parliamentary committees help Parliament control the executive?", a: "1. Departmentally Related Standing Committees and Joint Parliamentary Committees examine bills, budgets and policies in detail, which a full House cannot do.\n2. The Public Accounts Committee examines how public money has been spent.\n3. Committees question officials and ministries, which makes the executive accountable.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Describe the stages through which an ordinary bill becomes a law.", a: "1. A bill is introduced in either House by a minister (government bill) or another member (private member's bill).\n2. It is debated, often sent to a committee for detailed study, and discussed clause by clause.\n3. It is passed by that House and sent to the other House, which can pass, amend or reject it.\n4. If the Houses disagree, the President may call a joint sitting (Article 108) presided over by the Speaker.\n5. After both Houses pass it, it goes to the President for assent (Article 111); only then does it become an Act.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Compare the Lok Sabha and the Rajya Sabha with respect to composition, term and role in controlling the executive.", a: "1. Election: Lok Sabha members are directly elected by the people; Rajya Sabha members are elected indirectly by the elected MLAs through single transferable vote (12 are nominated).\n2. Strength: Lok Sabha at present has 543 elected members; the Rajya Sabha has at most 250.\n3. Term: Lok Sabha lasts 5 years unless dissolved earlier; the Rajya Sabha is permanent, with a 6-year term and one-third retiring every two years.\n4. Presiding officer: the Speaker presides over the Lok Sabha; the Vice-President is Chairman of the Rajya Sabha.\n5. Executive control: the Council of Ministers is responsible only to the Lok Sabha, which can pass a no-confidence motion; Money Bills originate only in the Lok Sabha.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: Rekha was elected to a State Assembly on the ticket of party P. Soon after, she announces that she is joining party Q, which has offered her a ministerial post. Party P's leaders complain that her action violates the law on defection.\n(i) What is such an action called?\n(ii) Which Schedule of the Constitution deals with it?\n(iii) Who can decide whether she should be disqualified?", a: "(i) Defection: an elected member leaving the party on whose ticket she was elected.\n(ii) The Tenth Schedule (anti-defection law), added by the 52nd Amendment (1985).\n(iii) The presiding officer of the House (the Speaker of the Assembly).", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "judiciary": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "High Court judges in India retire at the age of:", options: ["60 years", "62 years", "65 years", "58 years"], answer: 1, explain: "High Court judges retire at 62, Supreme Court judges at 65.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The law declared by the Supreme Court is binding on all courts in India under:", options: ["Article 124", "Article 131", "Article 141", "Article 143"], answer: 2, explain: "Article 141 makes the Supreme Court's declared law binding on all courts.", level: 1 },
      { q: "High Courts can issue writs for enforcing Fundamental Rights under:", options: ["Article 32", "Article 136", "Article 137", "Article 226"], answer: 3, explain: "Article 32 gives the power to the Supreme Court; Article 226 gives it to High Courts.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The Hussainara Khatoon case (1979) was about the rights of:", options: ["undertrial prisoners", "industrial workers", "property owners", "defecting legislators"], answer: 0, explain: "It concerned undertrial prisoners in Bihar and was an early public interest litigation.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which two judges were pioneers of public interest litigation in India?", options: ["H.J. Kania and M.C. Mahajan", "B.R. Ambedkar and Rajendra Prasad", "P.N. Bhagwati and V.R. Krishna Iyer", "A.N. Ray and M.H. Beg"], answer: 2, explain: "Justices P.N. Bhagwati and V.R. Krishna Iyer helped develop PIL.", level: 1 },

      { q: "Under the collegium system, the names of judges are recommended by:", options: ["the Prime Minister and the Chief Justice", "the Chief Justice of India with the four senior-most judges", "a committee of Parliament", "the Law Minister"], answer: 1, explain: "After the Second and Third Judges cases, the collegium of the CJI and the four senior-most judges recommends appointments.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Case: A dispute arises between two state governments over the sharing of a resource. Under which provision is it taken directly to the Supreme Court?", options: ["Article 32", "Article 143", "Article 131", "Article 136"], answer: 2, explain: "Article 131 gives original jurisdiction in disputes between states or between the Union and states.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Case: The President is uncertain about a question of law of public importance and seeks the opinion of the Supreme Court. Which statement is correct?", options: ["The Court must advise and the opinion binds the President", "The advice is sought under Article 136", "The matter must go to a High Court first", "It falls under Article 143, and the opinion is not binding"], answer: 3, explain: "Advisory jurisdiction is under Article 143; the Court's opinion is not binding.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): A judge of the Supreme Court cannot easily be removed from office.\nReason (R): Removal needs proved misbehaviour or incapacity and a motion passed by a special majority in both Houses of Parliament.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "The difficult procedure under Article 124(4) protects judges from pressure; no Supreme Court judge has ever been removed.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Which of the following does NOT help to secure the independence of the judiciary?", options: ["Salaries are charged on the Consolidated Fund of India", "Removal of judges needs a special majority in both Houses", "Judges are appointed by Parliament by a simple majority", "Courts can punish contempt of court"], answer: 2, explain: "The legislature has no role in appointing judges; that is what protects independence.", level: 2 },

      { q: "The Kesavananda Bharati case (1973) held that:", options: ["Parliament cannot amend Fundamental Rights at all", "Parliament has unlimited power to amend the Constitution", "courts cannot review constitutional amendments", "Parliament can amend any part of the Constitution but cannot alter its basic structure"], answer: 3, explain: "This is the basic structure doctrine; Golaknath (1967) had held that Fundamental Rights could not be amended.", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Public interest litigation has widened access to justice for the poor.\nReason (R): In a PIL only the person whose own rights are violated can approach the court.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 2, explain: "In PIL any person or group can approach the court on behalf of those whose rights are violated; that is why access has widened.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: A journalist writes a letter to the Supreme Court about bonded labourers working in a quarry, and the Court treats the letter as a petition. This is an example of:", options: ["public interest litigation", "advisory jurisdiction", "original jurisdiction", "impeachment of a judge"], answer: 0, explain: "The Court has accepted letters and news reports as petitions in PILs.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Which statement about judicial review is correct?", options: ["It is defined in Article 124 and applies only to the executive", "The Constitution does not use the term, but courts can strike down laws and executive action that violate it", "It lets the Supreme Court make laws in place of Parliament", "It can be used only on laws about Fundamental Rights"], answer: 1, explain: "Judicial review is the power of courts to examine whether a law or executive action is constitutional and to declare it invalid if it is not.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Which Article allows the Supreme Court to review its own judgments?", a: "Article 137.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "What was the 99th Amendment (2014) and what happened to it?", a: "It created a National Judicial Appointments Commission (NJAC) to appoint judges; the Supreme Court struck it down in 2015.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "What is judicial review?", a: "1. It is the power of courts to examine whether a law or an executive action is constitutional.\n2. If it is not, the courts can declare it invalid.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Distinguish between the original and appellate jurisdiction of the Supreme Court.", a: "1. Original jurisdiction: cases begin directly in the Supreme Court, e.g. disputes between the Union and states or between states (Article 131).\n2. Appellate jurisdiction: it hears appeals against decisions of lower courts in civil, criminal and constitutional cases, and can grant special leave to appeal (Article 136).", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Explain how judges of the Supreme Court are appointed.", a: "1. They are appointed by the President in consultation with the judiciary (Article 124); the legislature has no role.\n2. Under the collegium system, which followed the Second (1993) and Third (1998) Judges cases, the Chief Justice of India with the four senior-most judges recommends names.\n3. An attempt to replace this with the NJAC (99th Amendment, 2014) was struck down by the Supreme Court in 2015.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Why is an independent judiciary essential in a democracy?", a: "1. It settles disputes and protects rights of citizens against arbitrary action.\n2. It upholds the rule of law and ensures no organ of government goes beyond the Constitution.\n3. Independence lets judges decide without fear or favour, free from interference by the legislature and executive.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "State how the Golaknath case (1967) and the Kesavananda Bharati case (1973) differed on Parliament's power to amend the Constitution.", a: "1. Golaknath (1967): the Court held that Parliament cannot amend Fundamental Rights.\n2. Kesavananda Bharati (1973): the Court held that Parliament can amend any part of the Constitution, including Fundamental Rights.\n3. But it cannot alter the basic structure, and the Court decides what the basic structure includes.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Explain judicial activism and public interest litigation in India, with their merits and concerns.", a: "1. Judicial activism is an active role by courts in protecting rights and directing the executive, mainly through PIL, which began around 1979–80.\n2. In a PIL any person or group can approach the court for those whose rights are violated; the Hussainara Khatoon case (1979) on undertrial prisoners was filed by others on their behalf.\n3. The Court has even treated letters and news reports as petitions; Justices P.N. Bhagwati and V.R. Krishna Iyer were pioneers.\n4. Merits: wider access to justice for the poor, a more accountable executive, attention to the environment, bonded labour and prisoners' rights.\n5. Concerns: courts become overburdened, and activism can blur the separation of powers as courts enter areas of the executive and legislature.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Describe the structure of the Indian judiciary and the role of the High Courts.", a: "1. India has a single, integrated judiciary: the Supreme Court at the top, High Courts in the states and district and subordinate courts below.\n2. Law declared by the Supreme Court is binding on all courts (Article 141).\n3. The Supreme Court has the Chief Justice and up to 33 other judges; Supreme Court judges retire at 65 and High Court judges at 62.\n4. High Courts hear appeals from lower courts and supervise subordinate courts in their state.\n5. They issue writs for enforcing Fundamental Rights under Article 226.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: A lawyer reads a newspaper report that many undertrial prisoners have been in jail for long periods without trial. Although none of the prisoners has approached a court, the lawyer files a petition in the Supreme Court on their behalf, and the Court takes it up.\n(i) What type of petition is this?\n(ii) Who may file such a petition?\n(iii) State one concern about the growing use of such petitions.", a: "(i) A public interest litigation (PIL).\n(ii) Any person or group can file it in the public interest on behalf of those whose rights are violated, even if they do not approach the court themselves.\n(iii) Courts may become overburdened, or activism may blur the separation of powers by entering the area of the executive and legislature.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  }
});
